      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "New-generation drug-eluting stents have demonstrated the mid-term efficacy and safety, but possible differences between stents may emerge in a long-term period. We compared long-term outcomes of patients with chronic stable angina and an isolated de-novo lesion in the proximal left anterior descending artery that underwent percutaneous coronary intervention with Endeavor-zotarolimus eluting stents (E-ZES) and everolimus eluting stents (EES)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We prospectively enrolled 600 patients. Of these, 180 underwent E-ZES and 420 underwent EES implantation. Clinical follow-up was performed up to 7 years (median follow-up 61 months). The evaluated clinical outcomes were Target Lesion Failure (TLF), a composite of cardiac death, myocardial infarction and Target Lesion Revascularization (TLR), the Patient-Related Outcome (PRO) and stent thrombosis. Differences between groups evaluated with the Kaplan-Meier method and possible independent predictors with Cox proportional hazard regression."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "At 5 years, the cumulative probability for outcomes was: TLF: 13.8% versus 7.5%, p=0.025, cardiac death: 3.1% versus 2.5%, p=0.937, myocardial infarction: 1.2% versus 1.8%, p=0.829, TLR: 10% versus 3.3%, p=0.003, PRO: 19.6% versus 13.8%, p=0.528, ST: 2.5% versus 2.7%, p=0.965, for E-ZES and EES respectively. Differences between stents increased after 30 months. In multivariate analysis predictors of TLF adjusted for stent type were Diabetes mellitus and estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Both stents provided a favorable safety profile, with EES demonstrating better effectiveness. There was a late emergence in difference of endpoints after 30 months. Diabetes mellitus and eGFR predicted TLF."
          }
        ]
      },
